# Project headers

This directory contains project header files.
A header file contains C declarations and macro definitions that multiple source files can share.

Use the C preprocessor directive `#include` in a source file under `src` to include a header.
This works in C, C++, and other languages that support the directive.

```src/main.c

#include "header.h"

int main (void)
{
 ...
}
```

An include directive has the same effect as a copy of the header's contents in each source file that needs them.
Manual copies take time and can introduce errors.
A header keeps related declarations in one place.
When you change the header, programs that include it use the new version at the next compilation.
This removes the work of changing each copy and the risk of inconsistent copies.

In C, header filenames usually end with `.h`.

The official [GCC documentation about header files](https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/cpp/Header-Files.html) covers these topics:

- Include Syntax
- Include Operation
- Once-Only Headers
- Computed Includes
